    "innovation": "This paper provides a detailed multi-station analysis of temperature trends in Ghana (1983-2021), revealing highly localized and seasonal warming patterns that are often masked by broader, spatially-averaged regional studies.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "AgERA5",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SPATIAL_RESOLUTION",
            "target_entity_instance": "0.1\u00b0",
            "target_entity_type": "Resolution",
            "paragraph": "The Tmax and Tmin series were homogenised using the Climatol package Guijarro (2025) in R. This package was selected for its robust handling of missing values and comprehensive documentation. Daily estimates from the nearest AgERA5 pixel to each station were used to serve as reference series. The homogenisation process was first performed on monthly aggregates, which were subsequently used to adjust the daily records. AgERA5 was chosen due to its high spatial resolution (0.1\u00b0) and accessibility. While reanalysis data may contain systematic biases, they are generally considered more spatially and temporally homogeneous than raw station records, making them suitable references for this procedure (Guijarro, 2025).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Ghana",
            "source_entity_type": "Country",
            "relation": "IN_CONTINENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "Africa",
            "target_entity_type": "Continent",
            "paragraph": "Africa is uniquely vulnerable to the devastating impacts of climate change and has great diversity (Adeyeri et al, 2026). In Ghana, as in many other African nations, there is an urgent need for robust climate research to provide the empirical foundation necessary for informed policy-making and the development of effective mitigation strategies (Adeyeri et al, 2026; Pitman et al, 2011).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Asante Akim North District",
            "source_entity_type": "Country_Subdivision",
            "relation": "IN_COUNTRY",
            "target_entity_instance": "Ghana",
            "target_entity_type": "Country",
            "paragraph": "One key finding is that temperature trends in Ghana are highly localised, which is consistent with the spatially diverse patterns identified in coastal regions by Ankrah et al (2023). The specific manifestations of these trends varied considerably in both space and time (Figures 6\u201310 and 12). In general, annual Tmax and Tmin exhibited significant increasing trends across most locations, aligning with broader national assessments (Atiah et al, 2021; Oduro et al, 2021), as well as site-specific studies in the coastal zone (Ankrah et al, 2023), the north-east (Asamoah and Ansah-Mensah, 2020), and the Asante Akim North District (Baffour-Ata et al, 2023).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Upper East Region",
            "source_entity_type": "Country_Subdivision",
            "relation": "IN_COUNTRY",
            "target_entity_instance": "Ghana",
            "target_entity_type": "Country",
            "paragraph": "While Abbam et al (2018) reported non-significant Tmin trends in the Upper East Region, which is consistent with the findings for Navrongo in our study, some nuanced differences emerged regarding the Upper West Region.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Upper West Region",
            "source_entity_type": "Country_Subdivision",
            "relation": "IN_COUNTRY",
            "target_entity_instance": "Ghana",
            "target_entity_type": "Country",
            "paragraph": "While Abbam et al (2018) reported non-significant Tmin trends in the Upper East Region, which is consistent with the findings for Navrongo in our study, some nuanced differences emerged regarding the Upper West Region. Specifically, while Abbam et al (2018) found no significant Tmin trends for the region as a whole, our study identified significant increasing trends at the Wa and Bole stations, located in this region. Such discrepancies may be attributed to the use of gridded data at a resolution of 0.5\u00b0 by 0.5\u00b0 in their work, which potentially smoothed the finer details captured by the point observations used in our work.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Wa",
            "source_entity_type": "City",
            "relation": "IN_COUNTRY",
            "target_entity_instance": "Ghana",
            "target_entity_type": "Country",
            "paragraph": "Specifically, while Abbam et al (2018) found no significant Tmin trends for the region as a whole, our study identified significant increasing trends at the Wa and Bole stations, located in this region [Upper West Region].",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Bole",
